                <head>267. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>Contacts between the United States and the National Liberation Front (NLF)
                    regarding military issues occurred at the end of 1968. On December 19 the NLF
                    offered to release three American prisoners of war. Following meetings on
                    December 25, 1968, and January 1, 1969, between Viet Cong and U.S. military
                    officers in an area of Tay Ninh Province where a temporary stand-down had been
                    ordered, the three soldiers were released. Seven North Vietnamese seamen were
                    subsequently released by the U.S. Navy on December 16. Despite this successful
                    “exchange,” the U.S. and South Vietnamese Governments refused to recognize a
                    Viet Cong-sponsored New Year’s truce due to enemy violations of an earlier
                    Christmas truce. Documentation on the prisoner release is in the Johnson
                    Library, National Security File, Country File, Vietnam, 3 D (1), Prisoners of
                    War (General Material), 1/66-1/69; and in National Archives and Records
                    Administration, RG 59, Central Files 1967-69, POL 17-5 US-VIET S and POL 27-7
                    VIET.</p>
            </div>
